          "snippet": "Halpha_6563 at rest 6562.8 A. Hydrogen Balmer alpha (Halpha) line at rest 6562.8 A. Strongest optical emission line in star-forming galaxies. At z > 0.49 it leaves the DESI optical range (9824 A ceiling), so any z above about 0.5 must be anchored by other lines such as [OII] or [OIII]. Commonly confused with [OII] 3727 and [OIII] 5007 in single-line spectra; that Halpha/[OII] confusion is a leadin"
        },
        {
          "id": "line-degeneracy-single-line",
          "source": "https://arxiv.org/abs/2306.06307",
          "snippet": "The classic single-line degeneracy: one emission peak at observed wavelength L is consistent with several redshifts at once - Halpha gives z = L/6562.8 - 1, [OIII] 5007 gives z = L/5006.8 - 1, [OII] 3727 gives z = L/3727.1 - 1, and Lya gives z = L/1215.7 - 1. The Halpha vs [OII] confusion is the most common cause of catastrophic redshift errors in emission-line galaxies. Breaking the degeneracy re"
        },
